  "statement": "# Rotting Oranges\n\nA crate of oranges is arranged in a rectangular grid `grid`. Each cell holds `0` for an empty slot, `1` for a fresh orange, or `2` for a rotten orange.\n\nEvery minute, each rotten orange spoils its four orthogonal neighbours (up, down, left, right) that currently hold a fresh orange. Empty slots never change and never transmit rot.\n\nReturn the number of minutes until no fresh orange remains. Return `-1` when at least one fresh orange can never rot, and `0` when there is nothing fresh to begin with.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= grid.length <= 8` and `0 <= grid[i] <= 2`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "grid = [[2, 1, 1], [1, 1, 0], [0, 1, 1]]",
      "output": "4",
      "explanation": "Rot spreads from (0,0) one layer per minute: neighbours rot at minute 1, their neighbours at minute 2, and the last cell (2,2) rots at minute 4."
    },
    {
      "input": "grid = [[2, 1, 1], [0, 1, 1], [1, 0, 1]]",
      "output": "-1",
      "explanation": "The fresh orange at (2,0) is sealed off by empty slots and can never be reached, so the answer is -1 despite all other oranges rotting."
    },
    {
      "input": "grid = [[0, 2]]",
      "output": "0",
      "explanation": "No fresh orange exists, so zero minutes are needed even though a rotten orange is present."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function orangesRotting(grid: number[][])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "All rotten oranges spread simultaneously, so seed the queue with every one of them at time zero.",
    "Process the queue one level per minute and count a minute only when fresh oranges rot during that level.",
    "If fresh oranges remain after the queue drains, those oranges are unreachable: answer -1."
